    <name>
      <text>Using the first principles of differentiation, find the derivative of the function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Using the first principles of differentiation, find the derivative of \(f(x)\) with respect to \(x\).</p>
<p>\[f(x)= {@f(x)@}.\]</p>
<p>Recall: \( \displaystyle f'(x) = \lim_{{h \to 0}} \frac{{f(x + h) - f(x)}}{h}\) </p>
<p>\(\quad \quad \quad f^{\prime}(x) =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To differentiate the function \(f(x) = {@f(x)@}\) using the <b>first principles</b> (also known as <b>the limit definition of a derivative</b>). </p><p></p><p>We need to find the derivative \(f'(x)\), which represents the slope of the tangent line to the curve of \(f(x)\) at any point \(x\).</p>
<p>The first principles definition of the derivative is given by the following limit:</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle f'(x) = \lim_{{h \to 0}} \frac{{f(x + h) - f(x)}}{h}\)</p>
<p>For \(f(x) = {@f(x)@}\), let's plug this into the formula and simplify step by step:</p>
<p>\( \begin{align} \displaystyle  f'(x) &amp;= \lim_{{h \to 0}} {@difference_quotient@} \\
&amp;= \lim_{{h \to 0}} \frac{{@difference_quotient_expand@}}{h} \\
&amp;= \lim_{{h \to 0}} \frac{{@difference_quotient_factor@}}{h} \\
&amp;= \lim_{{h \to 0}} \left( {@expand(simplified_quotient)@} \right) \\
&amp;= {@ta@} \end{align}  \)</p>
<p>So, the derivative of \(f(x) = {@f(x)@}\) with respect to \(x\) using the first principles is \(f'(x) = {@ta@}\). </p><p>This means that the slope of the tangent line to the curve of \({@f(x)@}\) at any point \(x\) is \({@ta@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
